.TH V32LUA 1 "October 2026" "v32lua 20261019-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v32lua \- compile a subset of Lua to Vircon32 assembly and cartridge XML
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v32lua
.RB [ \-o
.IR output.asm ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-g ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-\-api
.IR api ]
.RB [ \-\-title
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-\-titleopt
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-diff\-opt\-title ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-build\-scripts ]
.RB [ \-\-rate
.IR hz ]
.RB [ \-\-fast\-circles ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-float\-as\-clearcolor ]
.RB [ \-\-xml\-preserve\-paths ]
.RB [ \-\-keyboard
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-\-mouse
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-\-bezel
.IR art.png " | "
.BR \-\-no\-bezel ]
.IR input.lua " | " cart.p8 " | " cart.tic
.br
.B v32lua
.B \-\-version
.br
.B v32lua
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B v32lua
compiles a purpose-built subset of Lua \(em global and local variables,
functions as first-class values, multiple assignment, the usual
statement/expression language, and a set of Vircon32-specific hardware
intrinsics \(em directly to
.B Vircon32
assembly. There is no bytecode virtual machine and no interpreter
involved at any point: Lua source is lowered straight to native
instructions for the target console's CPU, in the same spirit as
.BR v32c++ (1)
lowering a C++ subset to plain Vircon32 C.
.PP
A compile produces three files: an assembly file
(see
.BR \-o ,
below) and, alongside it, a cartridge XML definition (the same base
name with a
.I .xml
extension) and its twin for an optimized build
.RI ( Opt.xml ,
see
.BR "CARTRIDGE XML" ),
which record the cart's title, version, and any texture,
sound, and tilemap resources declared with
.B \-\-#
hints in the source (see
.B CARTRIDGE HINTS
below). Both are inputs to the ordinary Vircon32 build chain \(em
assembler, then
.BR packrom (1)
\(em which
.B v32lua
does not itself invoke.
.PP
.B v32lua
produces no output at all on success unless a verbosity flag is given
(see
.BR \-v ,
below) \(em this matches the Vircon32 C compiler's and
.BR v32c++ (1)'s
own behavior. A non-zero exit status, and a message on standard
error, indicate a genuine problem: a lexical, syntax, semantic, or
internal compiler error. Lexical, syntax, semantic, and internal
errors are reported with the offending source line reproduced
inline, pointing at the failure.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output.asm"
Write the generated assembly to
.IR output.asm ,
instead of the default. If
.B \-o
is not given at all, the output filename is derived from the input's
own name: its extension is replaced with
.IR .asm ,
so
.I game/main.lua
becomes
.IR game/main.asm .
The companion
.I .xml
cartridge file is always named from this same output path (its own
extension replaced with
.IR .xml ),
not from the input filename, so
.B \-o
also controls where the XML lands (unless
.B \-\-xml\-preserve\-paths
is given).
.TP
.B \-b
Build a Vircon32 BIOS instead of a cartridge. The program must define
.B "function error_handler()"
(run by the console when the CPU raises a hardware error) in addition to
.BR main ,
declare exactly one
.B \-\-#texture
and at most one
.BR \-\-#sound ,
and use the native vircon32 API. The generated assembly begins with the
hardware error vector and uses the BIOS program ROM page
.RB ( 0x10000000 ),
the XML
.B <rom>
element gets
.BR type=\(dqbios\(dq ,
and the generated build scripts pass
.B \-b
to
.B v32lua
and
.BR assemble .
.TP
.B \-g
Generate a companion
.I output.asm.debug
file mapping relative assembly line offsets back to the original Lua
source line and enclosing function name for each line of generated
code. Intended for step-through debugging of a compiled cart under
.BR v32sim (1),
not for anything read by the assembler or
.BR packrom (1)
itself.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report progress through the compiler's internal pipeline stages
(lexer, preprocessor, parser, semantic analysis, code generation) as
each one begins. No
.B \-v
at all (the default) produces no output on success.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Print additional internal/operational debug information as the
compiler runs. Unrelated to
.BR \-g ;
this is compiler-development output, not a source-to-assembly map for
the resulting cart.
.TP
.B \-w
Suppress all compiler warnings (for example, a missing
.B WAIT
inside a
.B main()
entry point, or a mismatched cart-resource ordering). Errors are
unaffected and still halt compilation.
.TP
.BI \-\-api " api"
Select the API layer:
.BR pico8 ,
.BR tic80 ,
or
.B vircon32
(native). Overrides a
.B \-\-#api
hint in the source. Without this option the API is detected: a
.I .p8
file is PICO-8, a
.I .tic
file is TIC-80, and a
.I .lua
file uses its own
.BR \-\-#api / \-\-#p8
hint or, with none, its entry points
.RB ( TIC()
selects tic80;
.BR _draw() ,
.BR _update() ,
.B _update60()
select pico8; otherwise native).
.TP
.BI \-\-title " title"
Cartridge title, used verbatim; overrides a
.B \-\-#title
hint. Without either, the title is the cart's own (TIC-80
.B \-\- title:
metadata, or PICO-8's first comment line) or the input file name, prefixed with
.B [PICO8]
or
.B [TIC80]
in those API modes.
.TP
.BI \-\-titleopt " title"
Title of the optimized cartridge (the \fIOpt.xml\fR twin); overrides a
.B \-\-#titleopt
hint. Without either, it is the cartridge title followed by
.IR " [optimized]" .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diff\-opt\-title
Give the optimized cartridge the very same title as the regular one (also
the hint
.BR \-\-#no\-diff\-opt\-title ).
An explicit
.B \-\-titleopt
or
.B \-\-#titleopt
still wins.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-build\-scripts
Do not write the
.I Make.sh
and
.I Make.bat
build scripts (see
.BR "BUILD SCRIPTS" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " hz"
Sample rate of the sound synthesized from a PICO-8 or TIC-80 cart:
.BR 11025 ,
.B 22050
(default) or
.BR 44100 .
Overrides a
.B \-\-#rate
hint.
.B \-\-p8rate
and
.B \-\-#p8rate
are the old names and still work.
.TP
.B \-\-fast\-circles
TIC-80 and PICO-8: draw filled circles larger than radius 31 as the
pre-rendered radius-31 disc, scaled (one GPU draw instead of about 1.2
per unit of radius; roughly 1.5% of the edge pixels differ from the
console's). Outlines are always exact. Same as a
.B \-\-#fast\-circles
hint.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-float\-as\-clearcolor
A Lua number given to
.B ioports.gpu.clear
or TIC-80
.B cls()
keeps its float32 bits as the clear color, as in older versions (a translucent
clear that smears moving objects). Off by default: numbers are converted to an
integer color word. Same as a
.B \-\-#allow\-float\-as\-clearcolor
hint.
.TP
.B \-\-xml\-preserve\-paths
Keep each
.B \-\-#texture
/
.B \-\-#sound
path in the XML as written in the hint (only the extension changes), write the
XML into the project folder instead of beside the output (the source file's
directory, or its parent when the source is in a folder named \fIsrc\fR and the
output in a sibling such as \fIobj\fR), and make
.B <binary>
the
.I .vbin
path relative to it. By default the XML sits beside the output and
holds bare file names only (\fIship.vtex\fR, not \fItextures/ship.vtex\fR),
because
.BR packrom (1)
resolves every path in it relative to the XML itself. See
.BR "CARTRIDGE XML" .
.TP
.BI \-\-keyboard " port"
Gamepad port (0\(en3) where a v32kbd keyboard is plugged in, for
.BR key() ", " keyp()
and
.B kbd.*
in native and TIC-80 mode. Default 2, the third port (the emulator's
"Gamepad 3"), leaving ports 0 and 1 for regular gamepads. Keyboard input
needs a v32io device (see
.BR "KEYBOARD AND MOUSE" ).
Same as a
.B \-\-#keyboard
hint; a program can also change it with
.BR kbd.port(n) .
.TP
.BI \-\-mouse " port"
Gamepad port (0\(en3) where a v32mouse mouse is plugged in, for
.B mouse()
and
.B mouse.*
in every API mode (and PICO-8's
.BR stat(32) \(en stat(34) ).
Default 3, the fourth port (the emulator's "Gamepad 4"). Mouse input
needs a v32io device (see
.BR "KEYBOARD AND MOUSE" ).
Same as a
.B \-\-#mouse
hint; a program can also change it with
.BR mouse.port(n) .
.TP
.BI \-\-bezel " art.png"
PICO-8: draw the side panels from your own art instead of the built-in
panels: a PNG (or
.IR .vtex )
with both panels side by side, 4:5 (for example 288\(mu360, or
96\(mu120 scaled 3\(mu). Overrides a
.B \-\-#bezel
hint.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-bezel
PICO-8: leave the screen margins beside the canvas black instead of
drawing the side panels. Overrides a
.B \-\-#bezel
hint
.RB ( off
or
.BR on ).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version, author, and project URL information, and exit
immediately without processing any input.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a brief usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The input was compiled successfully.
.TP
.B 1
No input file was given; an unrecognized or duplicate argument was
given; or a lexical, syntax, semantic, or internal compiler error
occurred while compiling. Details are printed to standard error, and,
for a source-position error, the offending line is reproduced inline.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile a single file, writing
.I program.asm
and
.IR program.xml :
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua \-o program.asm program.lua
.fi
.RE
.PP
(also writing
.I programOpt.xml ,
for an optimized build.)
.PP
Compile with the default output name (extension swapped for
.IR .asm ),
watching each pipeline stage run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua \-v game.lua
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile with a source-to-assembly debug map, for stepping through the
cart in
.BR v32sim (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua \-g \-o build/game.asm src/game.lua
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile an unmodified PICO-8 cart (API detected from the extension) with a
chosen title and lo-fi sound, and a TIC-80 binary cart:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua celeste.p8 \-\-title "celeste" \-\-rate 11025
v32lua game.tic
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a Vircon32 BIOS (it needs an
.BR error_handler() ,
one
.B \-\-#texture
and at most one
.BR \-\-#sound ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32lua \-b \-o bios.asm bios.lua
.fi
.RE
.SH ENTRY POINTS
A program must declare at least one of
.B main()
or
.BR game_loop() ;
its absence is a compile error.
.TP
.B game_loop()
If present, the compiler generates a continuous runtime harness: the
console calls
.BR game_loop() ,
issues a
.B WAIT
for the current frame, and repeats indefinitely. This is the usual
entry point for arcade-style games, analogous to
.B TIC()
in TIC-80.
.TP
.B main()
If present instead, the console halts once
.B main()
returns, mimicking a hosted C
.BR main() .
Continuous execution and frame pacing (including any
.B WAIT
instructions) are then the program's own responsibility; the compiler
warns at compile time if it does not find a
.B WAIT
emitted inside
.BR main() .
.TP
.B error_handler()
Required, and without parameters, when building a BIOS with
.BR \-b :
the console calls it when the CPU raises a hardware error, and the
generated assembly begins with a vector that does so. Without
.B \-b
it is an ordinary function.
.TP
.B init()
If present, alongside either of the above, it runs exactly once,
after top-level global RAM allocation and before the main loop
begins.
.SH LANGUAGE NOTES
This section is a brief summary, not a complete reference \(em see the
project's own README, and
.I doc/API.md
for the full hardware intrinsic surface, for the current, authoritative
picture, including known gaps under active development.
.PP
Supported: global and local variables, with a top-level
.B local
promoted to a global slot rather than a doomed stack local (its
storage would otherwise live in a frame that returns before any game
code runs \(em this applies equally to top-level locals pulled in via
.BR \-\-#include );
functions as first-class NaN-boxed values; multiple assignment and
variable swapping without user temporaries;
.B __asm__
(register/stack-preserving) and
.B __rawasm__
(unprotected) inline assembly, both supporting
.I {var_name}
string interpolation of Lua variables; a native, zero-cost hardware
intrinsic surface
.RI ( ioports.gpu.* ", " ioports.spu.* ", " ioports.inp.* ", "
.IR music.*/sfx.* ", " tilemap.* ", " system.* );
TIC-80- and PICO-8-compatible API layers, selected
per-cartridge with a
.B \-\-#api
hint; and a
.B \-\-#include
textual preprocessor for multi-file projects, resolved before parsing
begins.
.PP
Graphics conveniences in the native API:
.B ioports.gpu.clear()
takes no argument (reuse the current clear color), a preset name
.RB ( \(dqblack\(dq ", " \(dqwhite\(dq ", " \(dqblue\(dq ,
.BR \(dqred\(dq ", " \(dqgreen\(dq ),
a packed
.I 0xAABBGGRR
color (a literal, or a
.B hex()
value when it comes from a variable), or separate components as
.BI clear( r ", " g ", " b " [, " a ])\fR,\fP
each 0\(en255 with alpha defaulting to 255 (opaque) \(em folded to a
constant when all are literals, packed at runtime otherwise.
.BR spr() 's
eighth argument,
.IR blend_mode ,
accepts either a number
.RB ( 0x20 ", " 0x21 ", " 0x22 )
or one of the string literals
.BR \(dqalpha\(dq " (or " \(dqdefault\(dq "), " \(dqadd\(dq ", or " \(dqsubtract\(dq ,
resolved at compile time; for example
.BR "spr(id, x, y, nil, nil, nil, nil, \(dqadd\(dq)" .
.BI rgba( r ", " g ", " b " [, " a ])
returns the packed color word (components clamped to 0\(en255, alpha
255 by default), and
.BI color( n )
turns a number holding a packed color into the word; both are what
.BR spr() 's
color, the color form of
.BR clear() ,
and
.BR ioports.gpu.multiply / bgcolor
expect. A numeric literal written to an integer port is stored as its
exact 32-bit word.
.BI rect( x1 ", " y1 ", " x2 ", " y2 " [, " color ])
and
.B rectfill()
draw a rectangle outline / filled rectangle between two inclusive
corners, in that packed color (default white), drawn with the BIOS
texture's white pixel (texture \-1, region 256).
.BR key() ", " keyp()
and
.B kbd.*
read a full keyboard through a v32kbd device on a gamepad port (see
.BR \-\-keyboard ),
and
.B mouse()
(TIC-80's seven values, in every API mode) and
.B mouse.*
a mouse through a v32mouse device (see
.BR \-\-mouse ).
.PP
Explicit, permanent deviations from standard Lua, made to suit
Vircon32's freestanding environment: IEEE-754 float32 only, with no
Lua 5.3+ integer/float dual-number type \(em integers above 2^24 are
not exactly representable;
.B print()
requires screen
.B x
and
.B y
as its first two arguments; a bare
.B return
statement is a syntax error (give it a value); and all program
execution must occur inside a function reachable from
.BR main() / game_loop() .
.PP
Also supported: single-quoted and long
.RB ( [[...]] )
string literals, exponent number literals
.RB ( 1e\-3 ),
.B goto
with block-scoped
.B ::labels::
(forward references included), and string-library calls as methods on
string values
.RB ( s:sub(2,\ #s) ", " (\(dqx\(dq):upper() ).
Strings used as table keys compare by content.
.PP
A PICO-8 cartridge compiles directly: given a
.I .p8
file, only its
.B __lua__
section is compiled (line numbers still refer to the
.I .p8
file) and its
.BR __gfx__ ", " __gff__ " and " __map__
sections become the sprite sheet, sprite flags and map. A
.I .lua
program can take those assets from a cart with
.BR \-\-#p8\ \(dqcart.p8\(dq .
.PP
Also supported: numeric and generic
.B for
(with
.BR pairs / ipairs ),
.BR repeat ... until ,
closures with upvalues, variadic functions
.RB ( ... ),
multiple return values (a trailing call or
.B ...
expands in argument lists, table constructors and
.BR return ,
up to 32 values), metatables
.RB ( __index ", " __newindex ", " __call ", " __tostring ", " __len ,
.BR __metatable ),
the bitwise operators on 32-bit words, arithmetic on numeric strings,
.BI tonumber( s ", " base ),
and most of the
.BR math ", " string " and " table
libraries.
.PP
Not yet implemented:
.BR pcall / error / assert ;
.BR string.match / gmatch ;
.BR select() ,
and
.B next()
as a callable function;
the arithmetic, comparison and concatenation metatable events;
garbage collection (the heap only grows);
and a diagnostic for reading, from inside a function, a
.B local
declared in a block lexically outside any function at chunk level
(for example inside a bare
.B do...end
at chunk level) \(em such a read currently compiles silently to an
uninitialized global rather than the value the block appeared to
scope.
.SH KEYBOARD AND MOUSE
The Vircon32 console has no keyboard and no mouse, only four gamepads.
.BR key() ", " keyp() ", " kbd.* ", " mouse() ", " mouse.*
and PICO-8's
.B stat(32..34)
read a keyboard or mouse disguised as a gamepad, and get input
.B only
with one of:
.IP \(bu 2
the v32io hardware adapter (a Waveshare RP2350-USB-A board, R13 removed,
running the v32io firmware), which makes a USB keyboard or mouse appear as
a gamepad named
.B v32io:kbd
or
.BR v32io:mouse ;
it works with any Vircon32 emulator once a joystick profile is set up for
it;
.IP \(bu 2
a modified Vircon32 emulator that reads the computer's own keyboard and
mouse as v32io devices: the
.B v32io
branch of the fork at
.UR https://github.com/wedge1020/ComputerSoftware
.UE
(both the
.B v32kbd
and the
.B v32mouse
device), or the stock DesktopEmulator with the v32io project's
.IR emulator/v32io/v32kbd.patch ,
.IR emulator/v32io/v32kbd-text.patch
and
.I emulator/v32io/v32mouse.patch
applied.
.PP
By default the keyboard is read from gamepad port 2 and the mouse from
port 3; see
.B \-\-keyboard
and
.BR \-\-mouse .
.PP
Without one of these, no keyboard or mouse input is possible: programs
compile and run, but no key is ever down and the mouse never moves. A
regular gamepad left in the keyboard's or mouse's port is read as one.
See
.I doc/API.md
for the protocols and setup.
.SH CARTRIDGE HINTS
Cartridge metadata and resources are declared with
.B \-\-#
line comments, parsed by the preprocessor stage and used to generate
the companion XML file:
.BR \-\-#version ", " \-\-#title ", " \-\-#titleopt ", " \-\-#no\-diff\-opt\-title ", " \-\-#api ", " \-\-#texture ,
.BR \-\-#sound ", " \-\-#tilemap ", " \-\-#p8 ", " \-\-#rate ,
.BR \-\-#bezel ", " \-\-#fast\-circles ", " \-\-#keyboard ,
.BR \-\-#mouse ", and " \-\-#include .
The XML names the
.I .vtex
and
.I .vsnd
files made from each hint's
.I .png
or
.I .wav
file (same name; see
.B CARTRIDGE XML
for the folder).
A
.B \-\-#texture
or
.B \-\-#sound
hint both registers the resource in the XML and binds its name to a
compile-time constant usable directly from Lua; resource IDs are
assigned in source order and are guaranteed to match their position
in the generated XML.
.B \-\-#include \(dqfile.lua\(dq
textually splices another file in before parsing, with include-once
deduplication and cycle detection. The file is looked for next to the
including file, then in the current directory, then in each directory of
.B V32LUA_INCLUDE
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
then in the installed library directory, and finally in the library
directories next to the compiler itself (see
.BR FILES ); it performs no scoping transformation of any kind; a
.B local
at the included file's own top level is promoted to a global exactly
as in a monolithic file.
.SH CARTRIDGE XML
.BR packrom (1)
reads every path in the XML relative to the XML file itself. By default,
therefore,
.B v32lua
writes the XML beside the
.B \-o
file and puts only file names in it. With
.B "\-o obj/game.asm"
and
.B "\-\-#texture SHIP \(dqtextures/ship.png\(dq"
the file
.I obj/game.xml
contains
.RS
.PP
.nf
<binary path="game.vbin" />
    <texture path="ship.vtex" /> <!-- SHIP -->
.fi
.RE
.PP
so the
.IR .vbin ", " .vtex " and " .vsnd
files are expected beside the XML, in
.IR obj/ .
Textures and sounds that
.B v32lua
generates itself (PICO-8 and TIC-80 carts) are written there already.
When two assets collapse to the same name, the first keeps it and each later
one keeps just enough of its directories to be unique:
.I textures/ship.png
followed by
.I textures/extra/ship.png
gives
.I ship.vtex
and
.I extra/ship.vtex
(the latter expected in
.IR obj/extra/ ).
Only two assets whose whole paths collapse to one name (differing just by
extension, say) draw a warning.
.PP
Beside every XML a twin for the optimized build is written:
.I obj/gameOpt.xml
is identical except that
.B <binary>
names
.IR gameOpt.vbin ,
ready for the output of an assembly optimizer such as
.BR v32opt ;
no editing step is needed.
.PP
With
.B \-\-xml\-preserve\-paths
the hint paths are kept as written
.RI ( textures/ship.vtex ),
the XML goes into the project folder: the source file's directory, or its parent when the source is in a
folder named \fIsrc\fR and the output in a sibling such as \fIobj\fR (so
.B "\-o obj/game.asm src/game.lua"
gives \fIgame.xml\fR beside \fIsrc\fR and \fIobj\fR, where the build scripts go), and
.B <binary>
becomes the
.I .vbin
path relative to it
.RI ( obj/game.vbin ).
Hint paths are then relative to the XML too
.RI ( src/textures/ship.vtex ),
and generated assets are named relative to its directory.
.PP
The twin's title is the regular title followed by
.IR " [optimized]" ,
so the two cartridges can be told apart; set it with
.B \-\-#titleopt
or
.BR \-\-titleopt ,
or keep the two identical with
.BR \-\-no\-diff\-opt\-title .
.PP
With
.B \-b
(a BIOS build) the
.B <rom>
element says
.B type=\(dqbios\(dq
instead of
.BR type=\(dqcartridge\(dq ;
the rest of the XML is the same. A BIOS needs exactly one texture and at
most one sound.
.SH BUILD SCRIPTS
Along with the XML, \fBv32lua\fR writes the traditional Vircon32 build scripts,
.I Make.sh
(shell) and
.I Make.bat
(Windows batch; CR LF line ends), unless
.B \-\-no\-build\-scripts
is given. Run from any directory, a script changes into its own folder,
creates the output folders, runs
.B v32lua
again with the options that were given, converts each
.B \-\-#texture
and
.B \-\-#sound
file with
.BR png2vircon (1)
and
.BR wav2vircon (1)
into the place the XML expects it, then runs
.B assemble
and
.BR packrom ,
stopping with BUILD FAILED at the first error and ending with BUILD
SUCCESSFUL. It first checks that
.B v32lua
is in the \fBPATH\fR and says it is needed if not. The optimized build
.RB ( v32opt ,
.BR assemble ,
.B packrom
of
.IR gameOpt.xml )
runs only when
.B v32opt
is found; otherwise it is skipped.
.PP
The scripts go beside the source file, or in its parent when the source is
in a folder named \fIsrc\fR and the output in a sibling folder (such as
\fIobj\fR). A script that does not carry the \fIGenerated by v32lua\fR mark
is never overwritten. The default can be changed with
.B V32LUA_DEFAULT_BUILD_SCRIPTS
in \fIinc/config.h\fR.
.PP
Besides the options that change what is produced
.RB ( \-\-api ,
.BR \-\-title ,
.BR \-\-rate ,
and so on), the scripts repeat
.B \-b
on their
.B v32lua
line and pass it to
.BR assemble ;
together with
.B \-g
the assembler gets
.BR "\-b \-g program" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.lua
Input source, in the Lua subset this tool accepts.
.TP
.I *.tic
A TIC-80 cartridge (Lua carts only), compiled directly: its code and
bank 0's tiles, sprites, map, flags, palette, waveforms and SFX are read.
.TP
.I *.p8
A PICO-8 cartridge, compiled directly (see
.BR "LANGUAGE NOTES" ).
.TP
.IR *_pico8.vtex ", " *_sfxNN.vsnd
Generated next to the output for PICO-8 carts: the sprite sheet (plus
color swatches), and the cart's 64
.B __sfx__
entries synthesized into sounds (22050 Hz by default, see
.BR \-\-rate ).
.BR music()
is sequenced from them at run time.
.TP
.IR *_trackN.vsnd ", " *_sfxNN_nMM.vsnd
Generated next to the output for TIC-80 carts with sound data: each music
track the program can play, pre-mixed and looping, and each SFX rendered at
the notes the program plays it at.
.TP
.IR pico8_tone0.vsnd " .. " pico8_tone7.vsnd
Placeholder tones for PICO-8 and TIC-80 programs without sound data that use
.BR sfx() / music()
(an existing file of the same name is kept).
.TP
.I *.asm
Generated Vircon32 assembly, ready to be handed to the Vircon32
assembler directly.
.TP
.IR *.xml ", " *Opt.xml
Generated cartridge definition and its optimized-build twin, named from the
assembly output path (not the input path), referencing any
.I .vtex
and
.I .vsnd
assets registered via cartridge hints. Required by
.BR packrom (1)
alongside the assembled ROM.
.TP
.I *.asm.debug
Optional, with
.BR \-g :
a line-oriented map from generated assembly line offsets back to
original Lua source lines and enclosing function names, for use by a
debugger such as
.BR v32sim (1).
.PP
Texture and sound assets named by cartridge hints are not read by
.BR v32lua ;
convert them with
.BR png2vircon (1)
and
.BR wav2vircon (1)
before running
.BR packrom (1).
.TP
.I /usr/local/Vircon32/v32tools/include/v32lua/
The
.B \-\-#include
library: the standard-library ports from the source tree's
.I lib/
.RI ( string.lua ", " math.lua ", ...),"
installed there by
.B make sysinstall
or
.BR "cmake \-\-install" .
The directory is compiled into
.BR v32lua ;
a CMake build installed under another prefix uses
.IR <prefix>/Vircon32/v32tools/include/v32lua ,
or
.I <prefix>\ev32tools\einclude\ev32lua
on Windows.
.TP
.IR <dir>/include/v32lua/ ", " <dir>/../Vircon32/v32tools/include/v32lua/
Where
.B \-\-#include
files not found anywhere else are finally looked for,
.I <dir>
being the directory the
.B v32lua
executable is in: the layout of a release zip and of a Windows install
.RI ( v32lua.exe
beside
.IR include\ev32lua ),
and of an install under any
.I <prefix>
.RI ( <prefix>/bin/v32lua ).
So an unpacked release zip finds its own library wherever it is put.
.TP
.I inc/config.h
(in the source tree) Build-time defaults of the sample rates, side panels,
fast circles, keyboard and mouse ports, and warnings.
.TP
.IR Make.sh ", " Make.bat
Generated build scripts (see
.BR "BUILD SCRIPTS" ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B V32LUA_INCLUDE
Directories searched for
.B \-\-#include
files after the including file's own directory and the current
directory, and before the installed library directory: a colon-separated
list (semicolon-separated on Windows).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32c++ (1),
the Vircon32 C compiler and assembler documentation,
.BR v32opt (1)
(a separate, general-purpose Vircon32 assembly optimizer meant to sit
between this tool and the assembler).
.PP
Project repository:
.I https://github.com/wedge1020/v32lua
